 so is there  variation for that insect resistance  phenotype just as there's variation for  the acyl Sugar plant phenotype and can  some of that variation be attributed to  either the amount of a so sugar or the  chemo type of the a so sugars or some  combination of both  so to do that I did an insect choice  assay so a Western flower thrip was  exposed to to leaf disks and these leaf  disks were from the same leaf from the  same plant treated identically in every  way except that one was washed with  water and that temporarily removes the  foliar exudates by about 75% allowing me  to test presence/absence of a slow  sugars in a separate dimension looking  at differences among genotypes we have  celebrity and m82 which were included in  the experiment these are commercial  tomato controls they accumulate only  trace Asil sugars we have C 0 or 7 1 or  2 6 which is our acyl sugar benchmark  breeding line it accumulates about 15%  the level of acyl sugar of Solanum  Pinelli i la 7 1 6 we also have lines  that have a slow sugar level QTL that  modified the amount of acyl sugar we  have lines that modify but the Kimo type  of acyl sugars and importantly we have a  fatty acid 5 line and this line has a  Kimo type modulating QTL but it also has  a pleiotropic interaction with the acyl  sugar level that causes it to have a  level that's very low like celebrity and  m82 which gives us a very powerful and  closely related control to the rest of  the material here so here I am setting  up the the insect galleries the 2 leaves  Washington unwashed they're set in auger  so that the insect can only interact  with one side of the leaf disk then 10  female flower thrips 10 female Western  flower trees are inserted into the  center of the gallery and they're  allowed to feed and oviposit for about  24 hours after which point the leaf is  stained with McBride's solution d  stained with lactic acid and then under  the microscope I can count the eggs so  here's 1 &amp; 2 in this nice 